<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taiwan under Japanese rule</h1><p class="st-meta">1895 CE – 1945 CE · capital Taihoku (Taipei)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Japan's first colony, held for fifty years, during which Taiwan was transformed by railways, sugar and schooling under a strict colonial government.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> ceded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-china">Taiwan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Penghu</a> islands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> in 1895. Japanese troops overcame the resistance of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-formosa">Republic of Formosa</a> that year, but armed opposition by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> and indigenous groups continued for years.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A governor-general with wide powers ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taipei">Taihoku</a> (Taipei). Under Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodama-gentaro">Kodama Gentarō</a> and his civil administrator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goto-shinpei">Gotō Shinpei</a> from 1898, the colony carried out land surveys and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a>, built railways and ports, and developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camphor">camphor</a> for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>, while policing heavily. Resistance continued, notably the Tapani incident of 1915 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seediq-people">Seediq</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wushe-incident">uprising at Musha</a> in 1930. From 1937 assimilation policies pushed Japanese names, language and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan's</a> defeat in 1945, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China</a> took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in October 1945. The colonial legacy of infrastructure, education and public health, and the painful memories of repression and wartime mobilisation, have both shaped modern Taiwan.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taiwan-under-japanese-rule.jpg" alt="Taiwan under Japanese rule" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: kahusi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Japan_(1870%E2%80%931999).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1895 CE – 1945 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Taihoku (Taipei)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kodama Gentarō, Gotō Shinpei (civil administrator), Den Kenjirō</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese folk religion, Buddhism, State Shinto</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese, Hokkien, Hakka, Formosan languages</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/taiwan">Taiwan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Japanese Taiwan, Government-General of Taiwan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=taiwan-under-japanese-rule" aria-label="Taiwan under Japanese rule on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7903 1256 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taiwan under Japanese rule lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8223 1512L8529 1145M8223 1512L8221 1549"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8529 1145h0M8221 1549h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8223 1512h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8223 1512h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Taihoku. <a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=taiwan-under-japanese-rule">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
